# Optional vision backend — face and body landmarks, image segmentation, and cloth segmentation.
#
# To SET UP, not to regenerate: `py -3.13 tools/bootstrap.py` creates python-mediapipe/, installs the lock
# below into it with --require-hashes, fetches the models, and smoke-tests both detectors. The commands that
# follow are for regenerating the lock.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE ASK. `requirements.lock.txt` beside it is the ANSWER, generated by a
# real resolution and hash-pinned, and it is the one you install from. That split is the
# Python counterpart of `packages.lock.json`: CLAUDE.md §7 says a lock is updated as part
# of restore and its diff reviewed, never hand-written, and a hand-written Python lock has
# the same defect — it records what somebody believed rather than what resolved.
#
#     py -3.13 -m venv python-mediapipe
#     python-mediapipe/Scripts/python.exe -m pip install pip-tools
#     python-mediapipe/Scripts/python.exe -m piptools compile --generate-hashes \
#         --extra-index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu \
#         --output-file=src/vision/requirements.lock.txt src/vision/requirements.in
#     python-mediapipe/Scripts/python.exe -m pip install --require-hashes \
#         -r src/vision/requirements.lock.txt
#
# The install command lives here rather than in the lock, because pip-compile rewrites the
# lock's header on every regeneration and anything written there is lost. `--require-hashes`
# is the point of the exercise: it makes pip refuse any distribution — transitive ones
# included — whose hash is not listed.
#
# NOT required to build or run the studio. `bin/cli` works without it exactly as it works
# without potrace: the capability reports itself unavailable and a script can ask before
# committing to a route that needs it.
#
# Install into `python-mediapipe/` at the repository root — NOT `python/` (webapp) or
# `python-adk/` (ADK runtime). Those two are deliberately separate because ADK caps
# `websockets<16` while the webapp's lock resolves 16.1.1, and in one shared venv the
# second install silently wins and neither lock then describes what is installed. A third
# backend dragging in a large native tree has no business in either.
#
# **Version pinned to what PyPI actually publishes, checked rather than recalled.** An
# earlier draft of this file pinned `0.10.21`, which does not exist — the release line runs
# 0.10.20, 0.10.33, 0.10.35, then 1.0.0 and 1.0.1. A fabricated pin fails at install rather
# than silently, which is the mild version of this error; the habit that produces it is not.
#
# **1.0.x is a major bump from the 0.10.x line every tutorial and most of the linked
# documentation is written against.** It is the current release and the one to start from,
# but if the FaceLandmarker task API differs from the docs, drop to `mediapipe==0.10.35`
# and regenerate the lock rather than working around it.

mediapipe==1.0.1

# Cloth segmentation (U²-Net; the checkpoint is `models/cloth_segm.pth`, fetched with `--extras`). PyTorch's
# CPU builds, from PyTorch's own index, for two reasons.
#
# **Linux.** Plain `torch` from PyPI is a CUDA build there: torch 2.14 declares `cuda-toolkit` 13, cuDNN, NCCL,
# NVSHMEM and triton, several gigabytes the container never uses. The `+cpu` build carries none of them. On
# Windows PyPI's torch is already CPU-only; pinning `+cpu` on both platforms keeps one lock for both.
#
# **CUDA is per card, so it cannot be required.** Someone with a GPU installs the build for their card over
# this one, with the command pytorch.org's selector gives. The segmenter uses the GPU when torch reports one.
# The lock then no longer describes the venv, and the next `bootstrap.py` run puts the CPU build back.
#
# `--extra-index-url` above is what lets the resolver see the `+cpu` builds. Keep it in the generated lock's
# header (do not pass `--no-emit-index-url`), since `bootstrap.py` installs from the lock alone. With
# `--require-hashes`, a second index cannot substitute anything: every file must match a listed hash.
#
# **No `opencv-python`.** mediapipe already brings `opencv-contrib-python`, and both install into the same
# `cv2` folder. With both installed, the last one wins and uninstalling either breaks the other. The segmenter
# does not use OpenCV anyway.
torch==2.14.0+cpu
torchvision==0.29.0+cpu
